(* P6 -- key-compromise impersonation, responder side.
   The responder's own long-term scalar is handed to the attacker from the
   outset.  The question is whether that lets the attacker impersonate a
   third party TO the responder.

   The property is split across 25a and 25b because the two levels separate
   at exactly the point that matters.  Producing a well-formed message 1
   needs only es = DH(ei, Sr) and ss = DH(Si, Sr), both computable from the
   leaked responder scalar.  Completing the session additionally needs
   se = DH(Si, er), which requires the initiator's scalar.  WireGuard's KCI
   resistance lives in the gap between the two.

   25a asks the weaker question: has the responder committed key material for
   a session it attributes to si? *)

query si: G, sr: G, ki: key, kr: key;
      event(ResponderBegin(si, sr, ki, kr)) ==> event(InitiatorBegin(si, sr)).

process
  new siA: exponent; let SA = pk(siA) in
  new srB: exponent; let SB = pk(srB) in
  new psk: key;
  (* The responder's own static scalar is compromised. *)
  out(net, (SA, SB, srB));
  (* Bounded to a single initiator session.  With the responder scalar
     in the attacker's hands, unbounded replication exceeds the memory budget
     (documented in docs/model-notes.md).  One initiator session suffices to
     exhibit any impersonation that reuses a single honest run, and the bound
     is stated alongside the result rather than hidden. *)
  ( Initiator(siA, SA, SB, psk) | (!Responder(srB, SB, psk)) )
